Factory functions for deep test data, no library. Save /workspace/models.py:

from dataclasses import dataclass, field

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Address:
    street: str
    city: str
    country: str

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Customer:
    id: int
    name: str
    email: str
    shipping: Address
    billing: Address

@dataclass
class LineItem:
    sku: str
    qty: int
    unit_cents: int

@dataclass
class Order:
    id: int
    customer: Customer
    items: list = field(default_factory=list)
    status: str = "new"

    def total_cents(self):
        return sum(i.qty * i.unit_cents for i in self.items)

Building an Order by hand takes 15 lines, so people write lazy tests. Create tests/factories.py: make_address(**over), make_customer(**over), make_line_item(**over), make_order(**over) with sensible defaults, auto-incrementing ids via itertools.count, unique emails derived from the id, billing defaulting to a COPY of shipping unless given, and nested overrides that reach through (make_order(customer=make_customer(name="Ada"), items=[make_line_item(qty=3)])). Then tests/test_factories.py proving factory hygiene: two calls never share ids or emails; default mutable items list is not shared between orders (the classic aliasing trap; construct two orders and append to one); overrides stick; frozen dataclasses stay frozen (pytest.raises on assignment). Finally use the factories in tests/test_order_logic.py for total_cents with mixed items and an empty order. pytest green.

